    {
      "number": 1006,
      "source_pages": ["p0301#4"],
      "request": "Determine a such that 1 is a root.",
      "tasks": [
        {
          "id": "alg7-c4-ex1006",
          "part": null,
          "equation": "ax^2-3x-5=0",
          "domain": "a in R and x in R. The source does not assume a!=0.",
          "exact_result": "a=8",
          "derivation": [
            "The required substitution x=1 gives a(1)^2-3(1)-5=a-8=0, necessarily a=8.",
            "Conversely a=8 gives 8x^2-3x-5=(x-1)(8x+5), so x=1 really is a root; the other root is -5/8.",
            "The naturally degenerate case a=0 is the linear equation -3x-5=0, whose sole root is -5/3, not 1. It is excluded by the requested property, not by an invented hypothesis."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "number": 1007,
      "source_pages": ["p0301#5"],
      "request": "Prove that 1 is a root.",
      "tasks": [
        {
          "id": "alg7-c4-ex1007",
          "part": null,
          "equation": "ax^2-(a+c)x+c=0",
          "domain": "a,c in R and x in R. Neither a nor c is required to be nonzero in the source.",
          "exact_result": "x=1 is a solution for every real pair (a,c).",
          "derivation": [
            "Substitute x=1: a-(a+c)+c=0 identically. This proves the requested statement without division by a.",
            "The full factorization is ax^2-(a+c)x+c=(x-1)(ax-c).",
            "If a!=0, the roots are 1 and c/a. They coincide precisely when c=a, and the discriminant (a-c)^2 is always nonnegative.",
            "If a=0 and c!=0, the equation is -c(x-1)=0, a linear equation with sole root 1.",
            "If a=c=0, the equation is 0=0: every real x solves it, including 1. It is an identity rather than a quadratic with one distinguished root."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "number": 1008,
      "source_pages": ["p0301#6"],
      "request": "Find the coefficient relation for each prescribed root.",
      "tasks": [
        {
          "id": "alg7-c4-ex1008-a",
          "part": "a",
          "equation": "ax^2+bx+c=0 with a root x=-1",
          "domain": "a,b,c in R and x in R. No explicit a!=0 condition is supplied for this subpart.",
          "exact_result": "a-b+c=0, equivalently b=a+c",
          "derivation": [
            "Substitute -1: a(-1)^2+b(-1)+c=a-b+c=0. Thus b=a+c is necessary.",
            "It is sufficient because under this relation the polynomial is (x+1)(ax+c), which vanishes at -1.",
            "If a!=0, the second root is -c/a; it coincides with -1 when c=a.",
            "If a=0 and c!=0, b=c and the equation is c(x+1)=0 with unique root -1.",
            "If a=c=0, the relation also forces b=0 and the equation is an identity. These degree-degenerate cases need no new source condition."
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "alg7-c4-ex1008-b",
          "part": "b",
          "equation": "ax^2+bx+c=0 with a root x=c/a and the stated condition c!=0",
          "domain": "a,b,c in R; c!=0 is explicit in the source, and a!=0 is required for the given expression c/a to be defined.",
          "exact_result": "a+b+c=0, equivalently b=-a-c",
          "derivation": [
            "Substitute x=c/a: a(c/a)^2+b(c/a)+c=c^2/a+bc/a+c=c(c+b+a)/a.",
            "Since a!=0 and the explicitly stated c!=0 holds, this is zero exactly when a+b+c=0.",
            "Conversely, with b=-a-c, the polynomial factors as (x-1)(ax-c), so c/a is indeed a root and the other root is 1.",
            "The roots coincide if c=a. Both roots are nonzero because c/a!=0.",
            "The a=0 case is not a possible interpretation of this prescribed root: c/a is undefined. The c=0 case is explicitly outside the source condition; it would make the substitution zero regardless of b and cannot be used to infer the stated relation."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "number": 1009,
      "source_pages": ["p0301#7"],
      "request": "Find the parameter set for an equation with a unique root.",
      "tasks": [
        {
          "id": "alg7-c4-ex1009-a",
          "part": "a",
          "equation": "4x^2+28x+a=0",
          "domain": "a in R and x in R. The leading coefficient 4 is nonzero for every a.",
          "exact_result": "a in {49}; the unique real root is x=-7/2.",
          "derivation": [
            "A nondegenerate real quadratic has one distinct real root exactly when its discriminant is zero.",
            "D=28^2-4(4)a=784-16a. Setting D=0 gives a=49.",
            "At a=49, the polynomial is 4(x+7/2)^2 and its sole real solution is -7/2.",
            "For a<49 there are two distinct real roots; for a>49 there are no real roots. No value of a makes this equation linear."
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "alg7-c4-ex1009-b",
          "part": "b",
          "equation": "25x^2+ax+16=0",
          "domain": "a in R and x in R. The leading coefficient 25 is always nonzero.",
          "exact_result": "a in {-40,40}; x=4/5 for a=-40 and x=-4/5 for a=40.",
          "derivation": [
            "D=a^2-4(25)(16)=a^2-1600.",
            "D=0 is equivalent to a^2=1600, giving both a=40 and a=-40.",
            "The double root is x=-a/50. The two polynomials are (5x+4)^2 when a=40 and (5x-4)^2 when a=-40.",
            "For |a|>40 there are two distinct real roots; for |a|<40 there are none."
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "alg7-c4-ex1009-c",
          "part": "c",
          "equation": "ax^2+28x+4=0",
          "domain": "a in R and x in R. The source does not state a!=0; the quadratic and linear cases must be separated.",
          "exact_result": {
            "literal_unique_real_solution": "a in {0,49}",
            "quadratic_only_interpretation": "a in {49}",
            "roots": "a=0 gives x=-1/7; a=49 gives x=-2/7."
          },
          "derivation": [
            "First take a=0 without dividing by a. The equation becomes 28x+4=0 and has exactly one real solution, x=-1/7.",
            "For a!=0, it is quadratic and D=28^2-4a(4)=784-16a. A unique real root requires D=0, giving a=49.",
            "At a=49 the polynomial is 49(x+2/7)^2, whose sole real root is -2/7.",
            "For a<49 with a!=0 there are two distinct real roots; for a>49 there are none. There is no constant or identity case because the x coefficient is always 28.",
            "Therefore the literal condition on the displayed equation yields {0,49}. If the chapter context is intended to demand a genuinely quadratic equation, it yields {49}. The source itself does not state that extra restriction, so this ledger explicitly preserves the ambiguity."
          ],
          "fault_ref": "1009-c-degree-interpretation"
        },
        {
          "id": "alg7-c4-ex1009-d",
          "part": "d",
          "equation": "x^2+ax+114=0",
          "domain": "a in R and x in R. The leading coefficient 1 is always nonzero.",
          "exact_result": "a in {-2sqrt(114),2sqrt(114)}; x=sqrt(114) for a=-2sqrt(114), and x=-sqrt(114) for a=2sqrt(114).",
          "derivation": [
            "D=a^2-4(1)(114)=a^2-456.",
            "D=0 gives a=+/-sqrt(456)=+/-2sqrt(114). The double root is x=-a/2.",
            "The polynomials at these values are respectively (x-sqrt(114))^2 and (x+sqrt(114))^2.",
            "For |a|>2sqrt(114) there are two distinct real roots; for |a|<2sqrt(114) there are none."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "number": 1010,
      "source_pages": ["p0301#8"],
      "request": "Prove the reciprocal-root relation between the two displayed equations.",
      "tasks": [
        {
          "id": "alg7-c4-ex1010",
          "part": null,
          "equation": "Q(x)=cx^2+bx+a=0 and P(x)=ax^2+bx+c=0",
          "domain": "a,b,c in R with the source's explicit a!=0 and c!=0. All real roots of either polynomial are nonzero; reciprocal substitution uses x!=0.",
          "exact_result": "The real root sets correspond bijectively by s=1/r: roots(Q)={1/r : r in roots(P)}. Multiplicities are preserved.",
          "derivation": [
            "P(0)=c!=0 and Q(0)=a!=0, so neither equation has a zero root. Both reciprocal directions are defined.",
            "If P(r)=0, divide by the nonzero r^2: a+b/r+c/r^2=0. With s=1/r this is cs^2+bs+a=Q(s)=0.",
            "Conversely, if Q(s)=0, then s!=0 and division by s^2 gives c+b/s+a/s^2=0. Set r=1/s to obtain P(r)=0.",
            "The map r -> 1/r is its own inverse, proving full correspondence rather than only inclusion.",
            "For nonzero x, Q(x)=x^2P(1/x). If P(t)=a(t-r1)(t-r2), with roots repeated according to multiplicity, then Q(x)=a(1-r1*x)(1-r2*x)=c(x-1/r1)(x-1/r2), since a*r1*r2=c. Thus double roots remain double."
          ],
          "real_existence": [
            "Both quadratics have the same discriminant D=b^2-4ac.",
            "If D<0, both have no real roots. Their real root sets are both empty; the theorem does not assert a real root exists.",
            "If D=0, each has one distinct nonzero real root of multiplicity two: r=-b/(2a), s=-b/(2c). Here b^2=4ac!=0, so rs=1.",
            "If D>0, each has two distinct nonzero real roots, with the two-way reciprocal correspondence proved above."
          ],
          "exact_root_correspondence": "For D>=0, r_plus=(-b+sqrt(D))/(2a) corresponds to s_minus=(-b-sqrt(D))/(2c), and r_minus=(-b-sqrt(D))/(2a) corresponds to s_plus=(-b+sqrt(D))/(2c). Indeed 1/r_plus=2a/(-b+sqrt(D))=(-b-sqrt(D))/(2c), and likewise with the opposite signs. The rationalization uses b^2-D=4ac!=0, so no denominator vanishes. When D=0 the two labels coincide."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
